\par\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0  I always delight to ask a Yankee farmer, in field or road, whether he has any old crockery that he would be willing to part with. How he will skurry home \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93cross-lots,\u8221\'94 over the ploughed fields, or through the rows of growing corn, eager to pull out and sell his wife\u8217\'92s pantry treasures!}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 he calm and authoritative way in which \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Father\u8221\'94 is turned out of the \u8220\'93butt\u8217\'92ry\u8221\'94 when he tries to pull down from the shelf an old blue bowl or plate to sell. \u8220\'93Mother\u8221\'94\~14has kept her cinnamon-sticks and nutmegs for her apple-pies in that \u8220\'93Blue Dragon\u8221\'94 bowl for forty years, and she isn\u8217\'92t going to sell it now to please anyone.}
